# Architecture

```
fleet-live/
├── apps/api/          Express 5 + SQLite (node:sqlite)
├── apps/web/          React 19 + Vite + React Router
└── packages/shared/   Domain types, Zod validation, list/SSE contracts
```

Data flow: React `--HTTP + SSE /api-->` Express (`app.ts` + `routes/`) → controllers → models → SQLite.

`@fleet-live/shared` is the contract. Do not duplicate vehicle, list-query, telemetry, stream, alert, or driver schemas in api or web.

## Boundaries

- UI never accesses SQLite or API models.
- Models never take `req` / `res`.
- `apps/web/src/components/ui` is generic. Vehicle logic lives in `components/vehicles`, alert logic in `components/alerts`, plus hooks and `api/`.
- Live updates are SSE (`GET /api/stream`, `POST /api/stream/focus`), not WebSockets and not list polling.

## Current vs planned

**Implemented:** vehicle CRUD; server-driven list (search/filter/sort/pagination/facets); SSE telemetry with per-connection focus; telemetry history API; trips with the driven path as an encoded polyline (`GET /api/vehicles/:id/trips/latest`); vehicle list/detail/CRUD UI; Leaflet detail map (marker, SSE, trail from the trip); fleet map (`/fleet`, last positions in the viewport, live ticks for driving vehicles in view); route simulation with a city/highway speed profile and fuel consumption; simulation pause via `GET`/`PATCH /api/sim`; companies; session login (`POST /api/auth/login`); tenant isolation by `company_id`; roles (`dispatcher` / `viewer`); per-company retention for closed trips (`TRIP_RETENTION_DAYS`); alerts REST/UI (`GET`/`PATCH /api/alerts`, inbox `/alerts` with open/resolved and optional `type`, detail panel, resolve); live SPEEDING events from the ticker (8 s over the current sim road-class limit, one open row per vehicle, `ended_at` / `details`); drivers as entities (`GET /api/drivers`, `/drivers` UI); live speed indicator (`speedBand`: orange over `speed_limit_kmh` until the event opens, red while `speeding_open`, not OSM, no low-speed colour).

**Not implemented — do not treat as present:** frontend tests, CI/CD, trip archive, shift briefing, geofences, HU/licence dates, CSV export.

**Next (do this, not invite/CI):** Phase 6.3 — live LOW_FUEL from the ticker. Do not treat sim 50/120 as legal OSM limits. Details: `.cursor/ROADMAP.md` Phase 6.

The fleet map is `/fleet`, not the list page. After login it loads last positions for the session company in the visible bbox (`GET /api/vehicles/positions`). The driver picker is an optional view filter, not a requirement. Status chips and plate search apply to that company snapshot. If more than `FLEET_POSITIONS_MAX` match, `truncated` is set and no markers are drawn — not a sample of the first 2000 ids. `drivers` on the fleet query is a view filter, not a saved group. The list stays a query page. Unauthenticated vehicle, stream, sim, alert, and driver requests are `401`. Other companies’ vehicles and drivers are `404`, not listed. License plates are unique per company. Driver names are unique per company. SSE `vehicles-changed` and telemetry patches are delivered only to connections of the same company; `POST /api/stream/focus` cannot take over another company’s connection. `GET`/`PATCH /api/sim` pauses simulation for the session company only. Trips have no `company_id`; access is `trip → vehicle → company` (`TripModel.latestForVehicle` joins `vehicles`). Alerts have no `company_id`; access is `alert → vehicle → company` (`AlertModel` joins `vehicles`). Drivers have `company_id`. `company_id` on create/update is taken from the session, never from the request body. `dispatcher` may create/update/delete vehicles, `PATCH /api/sim`, and `PATCH /api/alerts/:id`. `viewer` gets `403` on those writes; reads (list, detail, map, stream, `GET /api/sim`, `GET /api/alerts`, `GET /api/drivers`) stay allowed within the company.

## Two layers of position data

- `telemetry` is the **hot buffer**: last known position for list and marker, plus the newest movement. Bounded by `TELEMETRY_KEEP_PER_VEHICLE` (100). Rows are meant to expire.
- `trips` is the **durable record**: one row per drive, the path as an encoded polyline (`@fleet-live/shared` `encodePoint` / `decodePolyline`). A 300 km haul is one row of a few KB. Closed trips older than `TRIP_RETENTION_DAYS` (default 90) are deleted per company (`TripModel.pruneClosedForCompany` joins `vehicles`; no `company_id` on `trips`). Open trips are kept. Prune runs after trip close and after a telemetry tick for companies in that batch.
- Never size the visible trail by a point count. That was the old bug: how far you could see depended on the tick rate, not on the drive.
- Appending stays O(1): `path = path || ?` in SQL, predecessor in `last_latitude` / `last_longitude`. The simulator appends the **road vertices** between ticks (`verticesBetween`), not the chord. Do not read the path back into Node per tick.
- `TripModel.close` simplifies with Ramer-Douglas-Peucker (`lib/geo.ts`, 12 m). `distance_m` keeps the sum of reported segments, not the length of the simplified line.
- Live map: SSE `path_delta` is an encoded suffix; the marker slides along the new vertices. Telemetry remains the hot position only. `path_reset` starts a new line (new trip) instead of concatenating.
- The simulator closes the open trip when it reaches a route end and turns around. The return is a new trip, not a second layer on the same path.

## Domain rules

- `status` is what the vehicle reports. The UI shows it read-only. Do not offer start/stop trip in the frontend — a real fleet does not decide that, and the simulator already drives `DRIVING` vehicles. Pause **this company’s** simulation with `PATCH /api/sim` (not the whole process).
- `DRIVING` opens a trip, any other status closes it (`syncTrip` in the vehicle controller). `TripModel.appendPoints` also opens one lazily, so seeded or imported driving vehicles still get a path.
- Ending a trip writes a final telemetry point with speed `0` (`TelemetryModel.recordStandstill`) and ends an open SPEEDING event (`SpeedingEventModel.endForVehicle`).
- `fuel_level` is measured while `DRIVING` (simulator) and manually maintainable otherwise. Do not overwrite a measured value with a stale form value.
- A trip has no per-point speed or time by design. Do not add parallel arrays; aggregates (`distance_m`, `max_speed`) live on the trip.

The `users` table backs login. `role` is `dispatcher` or `viewer`. Isolation is company, not driver. Drivers are a company-scoped entity (`UNIQUE (company_id, name)`); `vehicles.driver_id` is the FK, `driver_name` stays denormalized for search/forms. Alerts hang off `vehicle_id`; `GET`/`PATCH /api/alerts` joins `vehicles`. Inbox `open` is `resolved_at IS NULL` (independent of `ended_at`). SPEEDING rows are written by the ticker (`SpeedingEventModel`): 8 s consecutive over the current sim road-class limit (`speed_limit_kmh` / `speedLimitKmh`), one open event per vehicle (`ended_at` null), then `max_speed` / `duration_s` in `details` JSON; 2 s hysteresis or leaving `DRIVING` sets `ended_at` but not `resolved_at`. Every 8th simulated vehicle may exceed its class. Seed still inserts LOW_FUEL/OFFLINE only. Live colour is the same rule: orange while over the current class limit without an open event, red while `speeding_open`. Open alerts are the warning inbox; all alert rows (including resolved) are violation history on `/drivers`.

## Source of truth

- Code is what exists. If README and code disagree, code wins. README describes the running system and user-visible upcoming work. The engineering plan is `.cursor/ROADMAP.md` (local, not committed).
- `apps/docs/table.md` describes the table component, not a feature backlog.

## New work

- Reuse existing patterns before new libraries or layers.
- User-facing copy (UI and API `error`/`fields`) is German; machine `code` values stay English.
